Q: Simple p5.js sound player, sound is forever &#39;undefined&#39; A: <p>Supplementing the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79957320/6243352">existing answer</a>, you can fix the problem immediately by pinning your p5.js to 1.11.3. The only change in the snippet below to OP's code is <code>2.0.5</code> -&gt; <code>1.11.3</code>.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// this script is 100% unchanged from OP
let sound; 
let ply; 
function preload() { 

    // Initialize sound
    sound = loadSound("https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Computer_Sound_(Gravity_Sound).wav"); 
} 

function setup() { 

    //Checking playing or not
    var ply = sound.isPlaying();
    console.log(ply);

    playStopButton = createButton('Play');
    playStopButton.position(20, 20);
    playStopButton.mousePressed(playStopSound);
} 

function playStopSound() {
    sound.play();
    console.log(getAudioContext().state);
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- old import:
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/2.0.5/p5.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt; --&gt;

&lt;!-- new import: --&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.11.3/p5.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;!-- the above 1.11.3 is the only thing that's changed from the original post --&gt;

&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5.sound@0.2.0/dist/p5.sound.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Removing <code>preload</code> is a <a href="https://q5js.substack.com/p/p5js-preload-system-removed-from" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>massive</em> breaking change</a>, and I'm shocked that p5 apparently hasn't communicated it well, since it invalidates every p5 tutorial/code snippet that loads assets.</p>
<p>Given p5's target beginner-level audience, I'm a bit surprised they've introduced promises even if disregarding fallout from the breaking change. <code>preload</code> is a nifty trick to abstract away both promises and (most) callbacks, making the library that much more syntactically approachable. I can see beginners forgetting to use <code>async</code> or <code>await</code> and running into a ton of confusion.</p>
<p>p5 docs still reference <code>preload</code> everywhere at the current time:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/fzEY17i6.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/fzEY17i6.png" alt="screenshot of p5 docs showing preload and confusing ux" /></a></p>
<p>Even after clicking into the beta site, <code>preload</code> is still there!</p>
<p>I haven't dove into p5.js 2.x yet further than this, but if it's anything like Python 2 to 3, or many other major breaking bumps in various libraries over the years, it could cause years of confusion.</p>
<p>Pinning p5 to 1.11.3 for the foreseeable future could avoid a number of other footguns (or perhaps this is the extent of it--I'm not sure, but it's a poor first experience). Once the beta period graduates and docs are updated, then you can consider upgrading.</p>

Q: Scraping Google search result links with Puppeteer A: <p>For starters, I don't see results for the selector <code>.rc .r</code>. Google selectors aren't always served consistently across all locales, and can change in the future the code below will eventually require adjustment.</p>
<p>As of 2025, Google has implemented strict bot detection that defeats previous versions of this answer, as well as other answers on this page. Using a Puppeteer-managed browser will likely be detected and blocked. Consider using their API or another search engine.</p>
<p>Here's a version that connects to a regular Chrome instance to bypass detection. You'll need to update the <code>chromePath</code> and other variables to work for your system (mine is MacOS 14.4).</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const {exec} = require(&quot;child_process&quot;);
const puppeteer = require(&quot;puppeteer&quot;); // ^24.20.0
const {setTimeout} = require(&quot;node:timers/promises&quot;);

const chromePath =
  &quot;/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome&quot;;
const debuggingPort = 9223;
const userDataDir = &quot;/tmp/puppeteer-profile&quot;;

let browser;
(async () =&gt; {
  const cmd = `&quot;${chromePath}&quot; \
    --remote-debugging-port=${debuggingPort} \
    --user-data-dir=&quot;${userDataDir}&quot; \
    --no-first-run \
    --no-default-browser-check \
    about:blank`; // might not work: --headless=new \
  exec(cmd);

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 100_000; i++) {
    try {
      browser = await puppeteer.connect({
        browserURL: `http://localhost:${debuggingPort}`,
      });
      break;
    } catch {
      await setTimeout(50);
    }
  }

  const page = await browser.newPage();
  await page.goto(&quot;https://google.com/&quot;, {
    waitUntil: &quot;domcontentloaded&quot;,
  });
  await page.locator('[title=&quot;Search&quot;]').fill(&quot;stack overflow&quot;);
  await page.keyboard.press(&quot;Enter&quot;);
  await page
    .waitForFunction(
      &quot;document.querySelectorAll('.LC20lb')?.length &gt; 5&quot;,
      {},
      {timeout: 3_000}
    )
    .catch(() =&gt; {});
  const links = await page.$$eval(&quot;.LC20lb&quot;, els =&gt;
    els.map(el =&gt; ({
      title: el.textContent,
      href: el.closest(&quot;a&quot;).href,
    }))
  );
  console.log(links);
})()
  .catch(err =&gt; console.error(err))
  .finally(() =&gt; browser?.close());
</code></pre>
<p>Since headless is easier to detect than headed, you may need to run in headed mode and use <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/51883055/6243352">xvfb</a> on your server.</p>
<p>Another approach is to encode your search terms as a URL query parameter and navigate directly to <code>https://www.google.com/search?q=your+query+here</code>, saving a navigation and potential selector mishap. Here's an example of this using <a href="https://github.com/rebrowser/rebrowser-puppeteer" rel="nofollow noreferrer">rebrowser-puppeteer</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import puppeteer from &quot;rebrowser-puppeteer&quot;; // ^24.8.1

const browser = await puppeteer.launch({
  headless: false,
  userDataDir: &quot;/tmp/puppeteer-profile&quot;,
  args: [&quot;--disable-blink-features=AutomationControlled&quot;],
});
const [page] = await browser.pages();
await page.goto(&quot;https://www.google.com/search?q=stack overflow&quot;, {
  waitUntil: &quot;networkidle0&quot;
});
const links = await page.$$eval(&quot;.LC20lb&quot;, els =&gt;
  els.map(el =&gt; ({
    title: el.textContent,
    href: el.closest(&quot;a&quot;).href,
  }))
);
console.log(links);
await browser.close();
</code></pre>
<p>As of June 2026, the above rebrowser script succeeds both headlessly and headfully, but Puppeteer stealth fails in both environments.</p>
<p>See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79667013/6243352">this answer</a> for additional antibot options.</p>
